    "where": "https://github.com/bakobo/fiki/blob/keri-profile-v1.2/docs/keri-profile.md",
    "rules": "Canonical mode only. Refusals are named by the profile's section 9 codes. A verifier runs its checks in section 9's order and reports the first that fails, so every case has exactly one correct code. A refusal case carries one defect unless its note names two, and then it pins which of them section 9's order reports."
  },
  "encoding": {
    "body": "The content as a UTF-8 string, after any transfer coding is removed; null means no body is handed to the verifier, which is how a case tests what the headers alone announce.",
    "headers": "Field names as a sender would write them; a verifier matches them case-insensitively. Each value is one field line.",
    "covered": "Component identifiers in their RFC 8941 serialized form, as they appear in Signature-Input: \"@method\", \"@path\";req.",
    "effective_key": "The raw 32-byte Ed25519 public key, base64url without padding.",
    "signature": "The raw 64-byte Ed25519 signature, standard base64, as inside the Signature header's colons.",
    "seed_hex": "The 32-byte Ed25519 seed, hex, so a signer can reproduce every signature; Ed25519 is deterministic."
  },
  "policy": {
    "max_age": 300,
    "skew": 60,
    "request_minimum": [
      "\"@method\"",
      "\"@path\"",
      "\"@query\""
    ],
    "response_minimum": [
      "\"@status\"",
      "\"@method\";req",
      "\"@path\";req",
      "\"@query\";req"
    ],
    "body_rule": "Profile sections 3 and 9. A REQUEST's headers announce a body when it carries any Transfer-Encoding or a Content-Length other than a plain zero (one or more ASCII digits once SP and HTAB are trimmed, of value zero); a body so announced without a covered content-digest is insufficient-coverage at the covered list. A non-empty request body no header announced is insufficient-coverage at the body stage, after the signature and freshness checks. A RESPONSE has a body when it has content, judged at the covered list; its Content-Length is not evidence, since a HEAD or 304 response announces a length it does not send. A response to a request whose content was non-empty must also cover \"content-digest\";req; the request's headers do not count there, since both sides hold the whole request by the time a response is signed or verified.",
    "freshness": "Profile section 6: refuse created < now - max_age - skew or created > now + skew as signature-stale, then expires < now - skew as signature-expired. Each case gives the now it assumes, in seconds since the epoch; where it has a fractional part, the verifier rounds it down first.",
    "case_policy": "A case may carry a policy object whose fields extend this one: expected_keyid, the AID a client expects a response from (profile R1); authorities, the @authority values a verifier serves (profile section 3), whose presence also makes @authority required: a request that does not cover it is insufficient-coverage."
  },
  "keys_rule": "Profile R1. A keyid is a well-formed AID when it is the canonical qb64 spelling of one of keripy's PreDex prefix codes: B, D, E, F, G, H and I over 32 bytes; 0D, 0E, 0F and 0G over 64; 1AAA, 1AAB, 1AAI and 1AAJ over 33; 1AAC and 1AAD over 57; 1AAE over 114. The bytes are padded in front with zero bytes to a multiple of three, base64url-encoded, and the code written over the characters the padding became, so re-encoding gives back the keyid exactly. Any other keyid is malformed-key. A B keyid yields its key from the prefix, and the verifier asks no key store about it, so a B keyid this table does not list still verifies; that key must be a valid point of the prime-order subgroup or the keyid is malformed-key. A non-transferable code other than B is unsupported-algorithm. Every other keyid resolves through the key state the verifier holds, which this table stands in for: absent from the table is unknown-key, an entry whose effective_key is null is unsupported-signer, and an entry marked weak_key holds a key that is not a valid prime-order point, which is malformed-key as it is resolved. key_state is the state an implementation with a KERI stack reduces itself (profile R4): the current signing keys as qb64 verfers, and the signing threshold in keripy's form. The effective key is the one key that satisfies the threshold alone, when exactly one does.",
